<Base
  title="FFMI Calculator — Fat-Free Mass Index & Normalized FFMI"
  description="Calculate your FFMI, Fat-Free Mass Index, and Normalized FFMI using pounds and inches or kilograms and centimeters. See fat-free mass, fat mass, lean mass percentage, and BMI."
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "SoftwareApplication",
    "name": "FFMI Calculator",
    "applicationCategory": "HealthApplication",
    "operatingSystem": "Any",
    "offers": {
      "@type": "Offer",
      "price": "0",
      "priceCurrency": "USD"
    },
    "description": "Free Fat-Free Mass Index calculator. Calculate raw FFMI, normalized FFMI, fat-free mass, fat mass, lean mass percentage, and BMI from weight, height, and body-fat percentage."
  })} />

  <section class="hero">
    <h1>FFMI Calculator — Find Your Fat-Free Mass Index</h1>

    <p>
      Calculate your <strong>Raw FFMI</strong> and
      <strong>Normalized FFMI</strong> using pounds and inches or kilograms
      and centimeters. See your fat-free mass, fat mass, lean mass
      percentage, and BMI.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="calc-card" aria-labelledby="calculator-heading">
    <h2 id="calculator-heading">Calculate Your FFMI</h2>

    <div class="field">
      <label>Unit</label>

      <div class="unit-toggle">
        <button
          type="button"
          id="unit-lb"
          class="active"
          aria-pressed="true"
        >
          lb &amp; inches
        </button>

        <button
          type="button"
          id="unit-kg"
          aria-pressed="false"
        >
          kg &amp; cm
        </button>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="field">
      <label for="sex">Sex</label>

      <select id="sex">
        <option value="male">Male</option>
        <option value="female">Female</option>
      </select>
    </div>

    <div class="field">
      <label for="weight">Body Weight</label>

      <div class="input-with-suffix">
        <input
          type="number"
          id="weight"
          placeholder="e.g. 180"
          inputmode="decimal"
          min="66"
          max="660"
          step="any"
        />
        <span id="weight-unit">lb</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div id="height-imperial">
      <div class="field">
        <label>Height</label>

        <div class="height-grid">
          <div class="input-with-suffix">
            <input
              type="number"
              id="height-ft"
              placeholder="5"
              inputmode="numeric"
              min="3"
              max="8"
              step="1"
              aria-label="Height in feet"
            />
            <span>ft</span>
          </div>

          <div class="input-with-suffix">
            <input
              type="number"
              id="height-in"
              placeholder="11"
              inputmode="decimal"
              min="0"
              max="11.99"
              step="0.1"
              aria-label="Height in inches"
            />
            <span>in</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div id="height-metric" hidden>
      <div class="field">
        <label for="height-cm">Height</label>

        <div class="input-with-suffix">
          <input
            type="number"
            id="height-cm"
            placeholder="e.g. 180"
            inputmode="decimal"
            min="120"
            max="250"
            step="0.1"
          />
          <span>cm</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="field">
      <label for="bodyfat">Body Fat Percentage</label>

      <div class="input-with-suffix">
        <input
          type="number"
          id="bodyfat"
          placeholder="e.g. 15"
          inputmode="decimal"
          min="3"
          max="60"
          step="0.1"
        />
        <span>%</span>
      </div>

      <p class="field-help">
        Don't know your body-fat percentage?
        <a href="/tools/nutrition/body-fat-calculator">
          Estimate it with our US Navy Body Fat Calculator
        </a>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <button class="btn-primary" id="calc-btn">
      Calculate FFMI
    </button>

    <div class="result-box" id="result-box" aria-live="polite">
      <div class="result-primary">
        <div class="result-title">Normalized FFMI</div>

        <div class="big-number" id="result-normalized">–</div>

        <div class="result-unit">kg/m²</div>
      </div>

      <div class="result-secondary">
        <div class="metric-card">
          <span class="metric-label">Raw FFMI</span>
          <strong id="result-ffmi">–</strong>
        </div>

        <div class="metric-card">
          <span class="metric-label">Fat-Free Mass</span>
          <strong id="result-ffm">–</strong>
        </div>

        <div class="metric-card">
          <span class="metric-label">Fat Mass</span>
          <strong id="result-fat">–</strong>
        </div>

        <div class="metric-card">
          <span class="metric-label">Lean Mass</span>
          <strong id="result-lean-percent">–</strong>
        </div>

        <div class="metric-card">
          <span class="metric-label">BMI</span>
          <strong id="result-bmi">–</strong>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="ffmi-interpretation">
        <strong id="result-level">–</strong>

        <p id="result-interpretation">–</p>
      </div>

      <div class="ffmi-scale">
        <h3>FFMI Reference Guide</h3>

        <table class="result-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Normalized FFMI</th>
              <th scope="col">General interpretation</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>

          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Below 18</td>
              <td>Lower muscularity range</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
              <td>18–20</td>
              <td>Moderate muscularity range</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
              <td>20–22</td>
              <td>Higher muscularity range</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
              <td>22–25</td>
              <td>Very high muscularity range</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
              <td>25+</td>
              <td>Above the commonly cited Kouri reference point</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <p class="scale-note">
          These are broad educational reference bands, not medical
          classifications and not proof of natural or enhanced status.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="natural-limit-note">
        <strong>About the "25 FFMI" number:</strong>
        Kouri et al. (1995) reported normalized FFMI values up to about
        25 among the non-steroid-using men in their study. This finding is
        commonly cited as a natural-limit reference, but it is not a
        universal biological ceiling and cannot determine whether an
        individual uses performance-enhancing drugs.
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <article class="article-section">
    <h2>What Is FFMI?</h2>

    <p>
      <strong>FFMI (Fat-Free Mass Index)</strong> is a body-composition
      metric that expresses estimated fat-free mass relative to height.
      It has a similar mathematical structure to BMI, but FFMI uses
      fat-free mass instead of total body weight.
    </p>

    <p>
      This makes FFMI particularly useful for lifters and athletes who want
      additional context about muscularity that BMI alone cannot provide.
      FFMI does not directly measure muscle mass because fat-free mass can
      include bone, organs, body water, and other non-fat tissues.
    </p>

    <p>
      This calculator reports both <strong>Raw FFMI</strong> and
      <strong>Normalized FFMI</strong>, so you can see the underlying
      calculation as well as the height-adjusted value.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article class="article-section">
    <h2>How Do You Calculate FFMI?</h2>

    <p>
      FFMI is calculated in two main steps. First, estimate your
      fat-free mass from body weight and body-fat percentage. Then divide
      fat-free mass by height in metres squared.
    </p>

    <p>
      <code>
        Fat-Free Mass = Weight × (1 − Body Fat % ÷ 100)
      </code>
    </p>

    <p>
      <code>
        FFMI = Fat-Free Mass (kg) ÷ Height² (m)
      </code>
    </p>

    <p>
      For example, someone weighing 80 kg at 15% body fat has approximately
      68 kg of fat-free mass. At 1.80 m, their raw FFMI is approximately
      21.0 kg/m².
    </p>
  </article>

  <article class="article-section">
    <h2>What Is Normalized FFMI?</h2>

    <p>
      Raw FFMI uses height squared, while normalized FFMI applies a height
      correction to express the result relative to a 1.80 m reference
      height. This is particularly useful when comparing people with
      different heights.
    </p>

    <p>
      The Kouri et al. approach uses:
    </p>

    <p>
      <code>
        Normalized FFMI = FFMI + 6.3 × (1.80 − Height in metres)
      </code>
    </p>

    <p>
      A shorter person receives a positive adjustment and a taller person
      receives a negative adjustment. At exactly 1.80 m, raw and normalized
      FFMI are the same.
    </p>

    <p>
      The correction used here follows the method reported by Kouri et al.
      and should not be confused with calculators that use different
      normalization methods.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article class="article-section">
    <h2>FFMI Natural Limit: What Does 25 Mean?</h2>

    <p>
      The number <strong>25</strong> is one of the most frequently discussed
      FFMI benchmarks in bodybuilding.
    </p>

    <p>
      In a 1995 study, Kouri and colleagues calculated FFMI in 157 male
      athletes, including 83 anabolic-androgenic steroid users and 74
      non-users. The normalized FFMI values among the non-users extended
      up to a reported value of 25.0. 3
    </p>

    <p>
      This is the historical basis for the commonly cited
      <strong>maximum natural FFMI</strong> or "natural FFMI limit of 25"
      idea. However, the original study described its findings as
      preliminary, and the sample was limited to male athletes.
    </p>

    <p>
      Later research has reported FFMI values above 25 in athletic
      populations. In one collegiate American football study, 26.4% of
      participants had height-adjusted FFMI values above 25 and the
      97.5th percentile was 28.1. That study was not a natural-athlete
      sample, so it does not establish that those values were achieved
      without performance-enhancing drugs. 4
    </p>

    <p>
      Therefore, FFMI should be treated as a body-composition metric, not
      as a definitive test for performance-enhancing drug use.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article class="article-section">
    <h2>Is an FFMI of 26 Possible Naturally?</h2>

    <p>
      An FFMI above 25 is mathematically possible and has been observed in
      athletic populations. However, an FFMI value by itself cannot
      establish whether a particular person is natural or uses
      performance-enhancing drugs.
    </p>

    <p>
      Genetics, training history, body-fat measurement error, population,
      age, sex, and the method used to estimate fat-free mass can all affect
      FFMI. The 25 value should therefore be treated as a historical
      research reference rather than an absolute biological ceiling.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article class="article-section">
    <h2>Why Do Tall Lifters Need Normalized FFMI?</h2>

    <p>
      Height affects the relationship between body size and fat-free mass.
      Raw FFMI simply divides fat-free mass by height squared, while
      normalized FFMI applies a height correction to make comparisons
      relative to a 1.80 m reference.
    </p>

    <p>
      This does not make normalized FFMI a perfect measure of muscularity.
      It is simply a different way to account for height when comparing
      people of different statures.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article class="article-section">
    <h2>Do Women Use the FFMI Scale?</h2>

    <p>
      Yes. FFMI can be calculated for women using the same mathematical
      index of fat-free mass relative to height.
    </p>

    <p>
      However, the commonly cited 25 reference should not be treated as a
      universal female cutoff. The original Kouri natural-limit analysis
      used male athletes, while later research has reported sport-specific
      FFMI distributions in female collegiate athletes. 5
    </p>

    <p>
      One large study of female collegiate athletes reported an upper
      97.5th-percentile FFMI threshold of 23.90 kg/m², illustrating why
      sex- and sport-specific reference data can be more informative than
      applying a single male-derived number. 6
    </p>
  </article>

  <article class="article-section">
    <h2>Does FFMI Include Bone Weight?</h2>

    <p>
      FFMI is based on <strong>fat-free mass</strong>, not isolated skeletal
      muscle mass. Fat-free mass can include bone mineral, organs, body
      water, connective tissue, and other non-fat components.
    </p>

    <p>
      This is why FFMI should not be interpreted as a direct measurement of
      muscle mass. Some research calculates FFMI from components such as
      lean mass and bone mineral content, depending on the body-composition
      method used. 7
    </p>
  </article>

  <article class="article-section">
    <h2>FFMI vs. BMI: What's the Difference?</h2>

    <p>
      BMI and FFMI have a similar mathematical structure, but they answer
      different questions.
    </p>

    <table class="comparison-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Metric</th>
          <th scope="col">Uses</th>
          <th scope="col">Main purpose</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>

      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>BMI</td>
          <td>Total body weight + height</td>
          <td>General weight-to-height screening metric</td>
        </tr>

        <tr>
          <td>FFMI</td>
          <td>Fat-free mass + height</td>
          <td>Describes fat-free mass relative to height</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>
      If you want to calculate your general body-mass index, use our
      <a href="/tools/nutrition/bmi-calculator">
        BMI Calculator
      </a>.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article class="article-section">
    <h2>Why Body Fat Percentage Matters So Much</h2>

    <p>
      FFMI depends directly on your estimated fat-free mass. That means
      errors in your body-fat percentage can move your FFMI result.
    </p>

    <p>
      If your actual body-fat percentage is higher than the number you
      enter, your calculated fat-free mass and FFMI will be overstated.
      The opposite happens when body-fat percentage is underestimated.
    </p>

    <p>
      Don't know your body-fat percentage? Start with our
      <a href="/tools/nutrition/body-fat-calculator">
        US Navy Body Fat Calculator
      </a>
      and treat the result as an estimate.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article class="article-section">
    <h2>FFMI, Lean Body Mass, and Protein</h2>

    <p>
      FFMI is calculated from fat-free mass, so lean body mass and FFMI are
      closely connected. LBM describes an estimated amount of lean tissue,
      while FFMI expresses that amount relative to height.
    </p>

    <p>
      If you want the underlying lean-mass number rather than an index, use
      our
      <a href="/tools/nutrition/lean-body-mass-calculator">
        Lean Body Mass Calculator
      </a>.
    </p>

    <p>
      If your goal is muscle gain, use our
      <a href="/tools/nutrition/protein-calculator">
        Protein Intake Calculator
      </a>
      to estimate a practical daily protein target. You can also use our
      <a href="/tools/nutrition/macro-calculator">
        Macro Calculator
      </a>
      to turn a calorie target into protein, carbohydrate, and fat targets.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article class="article-section">
    <h2>How Accurate Is an FFMI Calculator?</h2>

    <p>
      The arithmetic is straightforward, but the result is only as accurate
      as the measurements used to calculate it. A major practical source
      of uncertainty is the body-fat percentage used to estimate
      fat-free mass.
    </p>

    <p>
      Different methods such as DEXA, skinfold measurements, tape-measure
      equations, and bioelectrical impedance can produce different
      body-composition estimates. Those differences can carry through to
      the calculated FFMI.
    </p>

    <p>
      For tracking changes, using the same measurement method under similar
      conditions can make trends more useful than comparing isolated
      results from different methods.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article class="article-section">
    <h2>Sources &amp; Methodology</h2>

    <ul>
      <li>
        Kouri EM, Pope HG Jr, Katz DL, Oliva P.
        <em>Fat-free mass index in users and nonusers of
        anabolic-androgenic steroids.</em>
        <a
          href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/7496846/"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >
          PubMed
        </a>
      </li>

      <li>
        Harty PS et al.
        <em>Upper and lower thresholds of fat-free mass index in a large
        cohort of female collegiate athletes.</em>
        <a
          href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31238804/"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >
          PubMed
        </a>
      </li>

      <li>
        Blue MNM et al.
        <em>Normative fat-free mass index values for a diverse sample of
        collegiate female athletes.</em>
        <a
          href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30893018/"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >
          PubMed
        </a>
      </li>

      <li>
        <em>Fat-Free Mass Index in NCAA Division I and II Collegiate
        American Football Players.</em>
        <a
          href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/27930454/"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
        >
          PubMed
        </a>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      RepMaths uses FFMI as an educational body-composition metric.
      It is not a medical diagnosis and it is not a definitive test for
      performance-enhancing drug use.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article class="article-section">
    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>What is a good FFMI for a natural bodybuilder?</h3>

      <p>
        There is no universal FFMI score that defines a good or natural
        physique. FFMI describes fat-free mass relative to height. The
        commonly discussed value near 25 comes from a 1995 study of male
        athletes and should be treated as a research observation, not a
        diagnostic cutoff or proof of natural status.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>What is the natural limit for FFMI?</h3>

      <p>
        The often-cited FFMI value of 25 comes from Kouri et al. (1995),
        which reported a well-defined upper value around 25 among the
        non-steroid-using men in that study. It is not established as a
        universal biological limit for every person or population.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>Is an FFMI of 26 possible naturally?</h3>

      <p>
        Values above 25 have been observed in athletic populations, but an
        FFMI number alone cannot establish whether someone uses
        performance-enhancing drugs. The original 25 reference came from a
        limited male sample, so it should not be treated as an absolute
        biological ceiling.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>Why do tall lifters need Normalized FFMI?</h3>

      <p>
        Normalized FFMI applies a height correction relative to a 1.80 metre
        reference. This allows raw FFMI values from people of different
        heights to be compared using the same height-adjusted framework.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>Does FFMI include bone weight?</h3>

      <p>
        FFMI uses fat-free mass rather than isolated muscle mass. Fat-free
        mass can include bone mineral, organs, body water, connective tissue,
        and other non-fat components, depending on how body composition is
        measured.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>How do you calculate normalized FFMI?</h3>

      <p>
        First calculate fat-free mass from body weight and body-fat
        percentage. Then divide fat-free mass in kilograms by height in
        metres squared. The normalized calculation applies a height
        correction relative to a 1.80 metre reference.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>What is the difference between BMI and FFMI?</h3>

      <p>
        BMI uses total body weight divided by height squared. FFMI uses
        estimated fat-free mass divided by height squared. BMI does not
        separate fat from lean tissue, while FFMI focuses on fat-free mass.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>Do women use the FFMI scale?</h3>

      <p>
        Yes. FFMI can be calculated for women, but the commonly cited
        natural-limit value near 25 came from a male sample and should not
        be treated as a universal female cutoff. Female-athlete research
        has its own FFMI distributions.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>

